00:19:06,600 --> 00:19:10,600
But as Adam mentioned, once that becomes available again, the scan will essentially kick off.

196
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:18,600
We can also organize our scanning machines into scanning pools, which is a way of grouping scanning machines or resources together.

197
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:21,600
And we can also tie them explicitly to sites.

198
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:26,600
So if we need dedicated resource for, you know, sets of applications, we can do so.

199
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:29,600
So we set up our team.

200
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:31,600
We know our scanning machines are good to go.

201
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:35,600
So now we can start to create our sites and the premise is very straightforward.

202
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:38,600
Our sites are our target applications that we want to scan.

203
00:19:38,600 --> 00:19:42,600
And then we run a scan schedule or ad hoc scans against those sites.

204
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:47,600
So typically a lot of your activity or configuration is done at the site level.

205
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:48,600
And then we just run our scans on top.

206
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:52,600
So we can see here we've got a lot of sites already pre-populated.

207
00:19:52,600 --> 00:19:54,600
They're organized into folders.

208
00:19:54,600 --> 00:19:59,600
As Adam mentioned, we've got some bulk action items that we can complete now.

209
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:03,600
So if I select the folder, it's then going to select all the sites within that folder.

210
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:06,600
I could do multiple folders and I can do a quick scan.

211
00:20:06,600 --> 00:20:07,600
I can move those sites.

212
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:08,600
I can delete them.

213
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:11,600
And this is just the start of our bulk action work.

214
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:17,600
So we've got some upcoming developments that are going to revolve around folder level actions.

215
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:26,600
So anything that you can typically do at a site level, for example, assign or amend the scan config, is also going to be achievable with the bulk actions as well.

216
00:20:26,600 --> 00:20:30,600
So as a new deployment, we might want to import our sites on mass.

217
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:34,600
We might have a lot of applications where we can complete the CSV template to do so.

218
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:38,600
But I'll just give you a quick flavor of what it's like to create a site from scratch.

219
00:20:38,600 --> 00:20:39,600
So we'll call this test.

220
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:42,600
Again, we can organize this into our created folders.

221
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:47,600
And then we want to complete our site scope, which is effectively what we're scanning against.

222
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:50,600
So one important component is that site URL.

223
00:20:50,600 --> 00:20:54,600
So this is the highest level URL that we want to include as part of that scan.

224
00:20:54,600 --> 00:20:58,600
And then all our sub directories will be included by default.

225
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:06,600
And we can see here that we're going to scan using HTTP and S or we can specify one of those protocols if required.

226
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:14,600
And if I expand the additional menu here, we can also have some inclusive and exclusive rules around those URLs.

227
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:16,600
So maybe we want to exclude part of our site.

228
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:17,600
We don't need to scan it.

229
00:21:17,600 --> 00:21:18,600
We don't want to.

230
00:21:18,600 --> 00:21:21,600
Or maybe we want to explicitly define some API endpoints, for example.

231
00:21:24,600 --> 00:21:27,600
And then we can start to apply advanced settings to our site.

232
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:32,600
So this is mainly going to revolve around scan configurations and authentication or application logins.

233
00:21:32,600 --> 00:21:36,600
So we give you a number of built in configs that you can use.

234
00:21:36,600 --> 00:21:41,600
So these effectively modify either the crawl behavior or the audit behavior.

235
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:45,600
Maybe we want to do something like minimize false positives.

236
00:21:45,600 --> 00:21:48,600
The choice is yours to use these as you kind of see fit.

237
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:51,600
And it's good to test them and see the kind of results you get.

238
00:21:51,600 --> 00:21:57,600
But you do have complete control, just like you do in pro, to create a custom config that suits your needs.

239
00:21:57,600 --> 00:22:01,600
If I leave it blank, it's just going to use its default settings.

240
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:05,600
So I might want an authenticated crawl.

241
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:06,600
So I need to add some application logins.

242
00:22:06,600 --> 00:22:08,600
So I've got two options here.

243
00:22:08,600 --> 00:22:10,600
One is a very basic username and password.

244
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:17,600
So for a simple login form, I can just supply those credentials and the crawler will use this to authenticate against the site.

245
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:18,600
Anything more complex.

246
00:22:18,600 --> 00:22:21,600
And I'm going to want to use my recorded login sequence.

247
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:30,600
Now this is a browser plugin or extension that lets me record the process of me logging into my site and navigating to it.

248
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:34,600
And that's going to generate a JSON file that you can cut and paste.

249
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:42,600
And again, it's going to essentially recreate the steps from this script of us logging into the application.

250
00:22:42,600 --> 00:22:47,600
So anything more complex, such as SSO, it might be a multi-form login.

251
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:51,600
That's going to be more beneficial from a recorded login sequence.

252
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:55,600
We've done some updates to recorded login since its initial version.

253
00:22:55,600 --> 00:22:57,600
It's on version two now.

254
00:22:57,600 --> 00:23:02,600
So we've added some enhancements around navigation and supporting particular elements on a login form.

255
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:04,600
For example, iframes.

256
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:09,600
And version three is also being worked on where we're specifically looking at pop-ups.

257
00:23:09,600 --> 00:23:12,600
Adam mentioned extensions.

258
00:23:12,600 --> 00:23:17,600
So this is where you would add your extension at the site level if you want to do so.

259
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:20,600
Again, we can provide a list of what's available.

260
00:23:20,600 --> 00:23:25,600
We also have our extensibility documentation if you've got any interest in developing an extension of your own.

261
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:31,600
Particularly if you like one in pro that's not necessarily available in enterprise, you know, you can look to create one.

262
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:38,600
And then finally, we can assign it to a scanning pool if you want to, again, use a particular piece of resource or that's tied to a particular site.

263
00:23:38,600 --> 00:23:41,600
So that's creating a site.

264
00:23:41,600 --> 00:23:43,600
That's kind of the main bulk of the work out of the way.

265
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:44,600
Now we want to do a scan.

266
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:50,600
So if I go to new scan, very simple, I'm going to pick from my site tree.

267
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:54,600
So my already created sites, the site that I want to scan against.

268
00:23:54,600 --> 00:23:55,600
And I've got two choices.

269
00:23:55,600 --> 00:23:56,600
I can run an ad hoc site.

270
00:23:56,600 --> 00:23:58,600
So as soon as possible, I run it the once.

271
00:23:58,600 --> 00:23:59,600
Good for a test.

272
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:03,600
But typically you're going to be scheduling your scans.

273
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:04,600
So date and time.

274
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:09,600
And then I can repeat this, you know, a number of days and then that might be forever.

275
00:24:09,600 --> 00:24:12,600
It could be repeatable a set number of times or until a particular date.

276
00:24:12,600 --> 00:24:16,600
And again, we've got some more work coming in terms of additional options.

277
00:24:16,600 --> 00:24:21,600
So for example, something along the lines of a blackout window where you don't want scans to take place.

278
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:25,600
For now, we'll just leave this at run once and as soon as possible.

279
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:31,600
And this is going to pull through all of our settings that we applied to that particular site.

280
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:37,600
So actually running a scan is very straightforward because we've already supplied all the required configuration settings.

281
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:39,600
We'll save this.

282
00:24:39,600 --> 00:24:45,600
That will then assign it to an available scanning machine, assuming one is available and it will perform our scan for us.

283
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:53,600
But if we go back to a previously run scan, same site, same configuration that we've just kicked off.

284
00:24:53,600 --> 00:24:56,600
And this is where we can view our scan results.

285
00:24:56,600 --> 00:25:04,600
So from a dashboard perspective, particularly at a site level, we're trying to build out a picture over time of our security posture.

286
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:08,600
But in terms of our individual results, we can start to delve in.

287
00:25:08,600 --> 00:25:12,600
All of our issues are reported by severity and confidence level.

288
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:15,600
So that's high, medium, low or informational.

289
00:25:15,600 --> 00:25:19,600
Then the confidence level is certain, firm, tentative or perhaps it's a false positive that we've marked.

290
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:21,600
Everything is color coded.

291
00:25:21,600 --> 00:25:25,600
Let's drill down into a particular issue that's been located at this location.

292
00:25:25,600 --> 00:25:27,600
And then we get our advisory.

293
00:25:27,600 --> 00:25:33,600
So we're going to get some issue detail, background information, remediation steps for the particular issue.

294
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:36,600
There will typically be a reference to our Web Security Academy.

295
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:40,600
So if you want to learn more about that vulnerability, that's a free resource available to you.

296
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:44,600
And also a sandbox environment where you can actually carry out that payload attack.

297
00:25:44,600 --> 00:25:49,600
And then, of course, the classifications that are associated with that particular vulnerability.

298
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:54,600
We can view the request and the response for that particular payload.

299
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:59,600
And then upon further investigation, if we determine that this is actually a false positive, we can mark it as such.

300
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:05,600
And there are effectively some global rules around this as how you want that false positive to propagate.

301
00:26:05,600 --> 00:26:07,600
So maybe it's just this one particular issue.

302
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:15,600
But if we know it's going to crop up for every scan, well, maybe we want to carry that across for the whole site or the particular URL that we're looking at here.

303
00:26:15,600 --> 00:26:21,600
Now, if we go back to our results, we can also start generating our reports.

304
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:29,600
Now, these might have been emailed at the end of the scan itself because we've had that configured, but you've always got access to the report.

305
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:33,600
Summary or detailed, these are HTML based reports.

306
00:26:33,600 --> 00:26:37,600
As I'd have mentioned, we've just started the release of our compliance reporting.

307
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:42,600
So we've got options here for OWASP top 10 and PCI DSS.

308
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:45,600
So if I select one of those, I can generate the report.

309
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:51,600
And this particular one will be formatted against the OWASP top 10 for 2021.

310
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:59,600
So folks, that's kind of scanning 101, particularly as part of the kind of trial or POC process.

311
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:05,600
You're going to be wanting to run lots of tests, start getting used to scan configurations and analyzing results.

312
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:12,600
We've got sites that we can provide specifically for this purpose as well, if you're not just testing against internal applications.

313
00:27:12,600 --> 00:27:21,600
But at that stage, once you're more comfortable with the interface and looking at results, you're probably going to want to start to build out your installation.

314
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:26,600
So this is typically around integrations. So maybe you want to start doing things like issue tracking.

315
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:30,600
So we've got elements around JIRA, GitLab and Trello integration.

316
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:36,600
And these are for either manual or automated tickets based on our results that we get from scans.

317
00:27:36,600 --> 00:27:40,600
We've relatively recently released our Slack integration as well.

318
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:48,600
So these are notifications in relation to scans, scans finishing, kicking off, failing issues, etc.

319
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:55,600
Now, one element that Adam mentioned for quite a strong use case for enterprise is CI CD pipeline integration.

