00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:06,600
So the Burp scan is essentially a sort of our older method, how we originally started doing it.

330
00:29:06,600 --> 00:29:15,600
Site driven scan is useful because it's going to take all of that pre-populated configuration data you've already done for your site and just kick the scan off based on that.

331
00:29:15,600 --> 00:29:23,600
So if we go to configure this particular project, you can see here as a build step, we've now got this option for Burp site driven scan.

332
00:29:23,600 --> 00:29:28,600
So if I just go to another, you can see we've now got these two options as a result of installing the driver.

333
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:33,600
It's very straightforward. So we want to connect back to our enterprise server.

334
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:40,600
So it's a URL and port number. We've generated an API key from an API user within Burp Suite Enterprise.

335
00:29:40,600 --> 00:29:44,600
This is how we're going to interact and authenticate with our enterprise installation.

336
00:29:44,600 --> 00:29:49,600
And then because those are now valid credentials, we populate our site tree.

337
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:54,600
So all of those sites that we pre-configured, we can now initiate pipeline scans against.

338
00:29:54,600 --> 00:29:59,600
So we're very simply select one. So we're looking at PortsWiggleLabs earlier.

339
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:04,600
And then we say, well, what's the severity level where we want the build to fail and also the confidence level?

